body:
## Do

- Make the `memory` MCP server. It holds all four memory tools.
- Make a new server for each session, built with that session's user and trip. No memory tool takes a user ID or trip ID as an input, so an agent can't reach another user's memory, even with a made-up ID (tool design, section 4).
- Follow the `trip` server's code (PR #44). `createTripServer()` in `src/tools/trip-server.ts` makes the server for one session from a `TripSession` (`src/tools/trip-session.ts`). The tools and their tests are in `src/tools/trip-brief-tools.ts` and `trip-brief-tools.test.ts`.

## Done when

- Tests with fake repositories (DEVELOPMENT.md section 5, such as `src/persistence/fake-memory-store.ts`) cover the four tools as tool design section 2.2 describes them. Examples: one user can't read, update, or forget another user's items through them, and the error is the same as for an ID that doesn't exist. An add over the maximum memory items or the maximum memory item length (technical spec section 10) is refused, and the error gives the limit.
- The PR shows the server's description, and each tool's description, input schema, and an example of what it returns. As in PR #44, take them from an MCP client connected to the real server object, so the schema is the JSON Schema the model sees.

Closes #47

## Results

- [x] **Tests with fake repositories cover the four tools as tool design section 2.2 describes them.** `src/tools/memory-tools.test.ts` covers: `read_memory` on empty memory gives the counts and limits; it lists each item with its ID, user or trip, and text, in the order added; it leaves out another user's memory and another trip's memory; user memory shows in every trip of the same user. `add_memory_item` adds to user or trip memory and gives the new ID; an add over the maximum items is refused, gives the limit, and adds nothing, while trip memory keeps its own limit; text over the maximum length is refused with the limit; empty text is refused; a trip item on a started trip is refused, and a user item still goes through. `update_memory_item` replaces the text and keeps the ID and memory; another user's item gets the same error as an ID that doesn't exist, and is left alone; the user's own item from another trip gets the same error; text over the maximum length is refused with the limit; an ID of another kind says so. `forget_memory_item` removes the item; another user's item gets the same error as an ID that doesn't exist, and stays; a trip item on a started trip is refused, and a user item is still forgotten. `src/tools/memory-server.test.ts` goes through an MCP client connected to the real server: it lists the four tools with no user or trip ID, and schemas that refuse other keys; the limits show in what the model sees; an add that names a `userId` is refused, and saves nothing. `npm run check` passes: 77 files, 591 tests.
- [x] **The PR shows the server's description, and each tool's description, input schema, and an example of what it returns.** Below. It comes from a run against the local Postgres, through an MCP client connected to the real server object, so the schema is the JSON Schema the model sees.

Checked by hand against the local Postgres, with two test users, each with a new trip: after the calls below, the `memory_items` table held user B's "Posh hotels only." unchanged, and user A's lodge item with its new text. The refused adds left no row, and user A's forgotten item was gone. The test rows and trips were deleted afterwards.

### Server description (`memory`)

> Once the trip has started, its trip memory can't change, and user memory still can. When a change is refused, nothing is changed, and the error says why.

### Tools, as `tools/list` returns them

```json
